body:
The International Union Against Tuberculosis and Lung Disease (The Union) has published a report on access to inhaled medicines, asking why progress made on insulin has not been matched for inhalers. The organisation announced it in a LinkedIn post.

The report, titled "Enhancing Access to Inhaled Medicines: Lessons from Insulin Access", was produced with the Forum of International Respiratory Societies and the NCD Policy Lab. According to The Union, it was written by Alstonia Impact and funded by the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation.

The Union said the world has made significant progress in improving access to insulin for people with diabetes, through political commitment, health system investment, industry engagement and community advocacy, but is "still falling behind" on inhalers. It cited figures of 750 million people living with asthma or COPD worldwide and 3.8 million deaths a year from these conditions. It said deaths from chronic respiratory diseases are projected to exceed 8 million annually by 2050. In many countries, it said, the inhalers people need are either unavailable or unaffordable.

The report outlines five actions:

- turning political commitments into funded care;
- updating treatment guidance and training health workers;
- testing and scaling proven programmes;
- negotiating affordable prices and securing supply chains;
- strengthening community voices and challenging stigma.

The Union described the situation as "a failure of humanity" rather than of science, and said every person, everywhere, deserves access to medicines that help them breathe, live and thrive.
